1:1:comment:spaced:// Comments become a single space before directives are recognized, as
2:1:comment:spaced:// "g++ -E" and "clang++ -E" show. A '#' after a comment that spans lines
3:1:comment:spaced:// is not at the start of a line, so it does not begin a directive:
4:1:identifier:spaced:int
4:5:identifier:spaced:x
4:6:punctuator:joined:;
4:8:comment:spaced:/* comment\n*/
5:4:punctuator:spaced:#
5:5:identifier:joined:define
5:12:identifier:spaced:CHECK
5:17:punctuator:joined:(
5:18:identifier:joined:x
5:19:punctuator:joined:)
5:21:identifier:spaced:x
6:1:comment:spaced:// And a directive goes on through a comment that spans lines, so the
7:1:comment:spaced:// declaration after it belongs to the macro:
8:1:preprocessor:spaced:#define FOO /* comment\n*/ int *ptr;
10:1:identifier:spaced:int
10:5:identifier:spaced:after
10:10:punctuator:joined:;
